Velocity-dependent hypertonia, Hyperreflexia with clonus, Positive Babinski sign

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-057
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of involuntary, painful muscle spasms and limb rigidity causing extreme distress and an inability to transfer from bed.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and primary caregiver
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with an established central nervous system lesion experiencing a sudden, terrifying escalation in severe, painful flexor spasms and total limb rigidity. Urgent assessment was strictly mandated to rapidly evaluate for acute systemic triggers (e.g., occult urinary tract infection, pressure injury) exacerbating the spasticity, and to administer immediate, potent anti-spasmodic medications to break the painful cycle prior to daytime specialist review.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My legs keep violently pulling up into my chest; the cramping is agonizing, and they are so rigid my carer cannot even dress me."
History of Present Illness: Patient with a known history of remote spinal cord injury presents with an acute, severe exacerbation of lower limb spasticity. Timeline: Baseline mild hypertonia abruptly worsened over the past 24 hours. Currently reports continuous, involuntary, and exceedingly painful flexor muscle spasms unpredictably triggered by any sudden cutaneous stimuli, such as bedsheets touching the skin. Patient describes an abnormal, fixed resting posture of lower extremity extension that violently transitions into flexion spasms upon movement. Severe impaired fine motor dexterity and slowed voluntary movement entirely disproportionate to the absolute degree of muscle weakness. The spasms are preventing any sleep and have made routine transfers by the caregiver impossible.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound; completely rigid lower limbs preventing transfers to the wheelchair.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver strain and inability to perform essential hygiene care due to limb rigidity; severe pain and sleep deprivation for the patient.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 146/92 | HR: 102 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, in visible severe distress, grimacing as the lower limbs intermittently spasm into flexion.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals profound upper motor neuron signs. Velocity-dependent increase in muscle tone observed, demonstrating significantly greater resistance to rapid passive stretch than slow stretch in the lower limbs. Distinct clasp-knife phenomenon characterized by initial high resistance to passive stretch of the quadriceps followed by a sudden, yielding release. Exaggerated deep tendon reflexes (3+ to 4+) uniformly present across both lower limbs. Presence of sustained rhythmic clonus (>5 beats) elicited at both ankles. Positive Babinski sign bilaterally, demonstrating a pathological upgoing plantar reflex with fanning of the toes. Careful skin and systemic examination reveals a slightly cloudy, strong-smelling urine output in the catheter bag and a low-grade fever, suggesting a likely infective trigger for the acute spasticity flare.
Environmental Observations: Wheelchair empty beside the bed; caregiver visibly exhausted.
Medication Reconciliation: Baseline Baclofen taken as prescribed, completely inadequate for this acute crisis.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute severe exacerbation of Spasticity secondary to suspected Urinary Tract Infection - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Acute Spinal Cord Compression (New lesion) - Unlikely given the established remote injury and lack of a distinct new sensory level shift, but the systemic trigger is the primary driver here.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out by the bilateral, fluctuating spastic nature of the presentation, absence of unilateral limb swelling, and normal capillary refill.

Seizure Activity (Focal) - Ruled out by fully preserved consciousness and the classic velocity-dependent tone and clasp-knife properties of the spasms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in a severe, painful crisis driven by profound central hyperreflexia and spasticity. The critical clinical task in any acute flare of established spasticity is to identify the underlying noxious systemic trigger (the "driver"). Examination definitively confirmed massive upper motor neuron hypertonia (clonus, Babinski, velocity-dependent resistance) and identified a highly suspicious clinical picture for a urinary tract infection (fever, cloudy urine) acting as the acute irritant. Postponing evaluation would have left the patient locked in agonizing spasms, unable to be cared for, and allowed the occult infection to progress. Urgent after-hours assessment safely broke the spasm cycle with targeted medication, identified the likely systemic trigger, and initiated immediate antibiotic therapy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Diazepam 5mg STAT to immediately break the acute, painful skeletal muscle spasms via central GABAergic inhibition. Prescribed oral Cephalexin 500mg QID for 5 days pending urine culture results. Adjusted baseline Baclofen dosing slightly upwards for the next 72 hours.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Perform slow, sustained, extremely gentle passive stretching of the legs for 10 minutes prior to attempting any transfers. Consequence: The slow stretch avoids triggering the velocity-dependent reflex loop, allowing the muscles to safely lengthen without snapping back into an agonizing spasm (negative reinforcement).
Action: Ensure fluid intake is increased to 2.5 liters daily while treating the urinary tract infection. Consequence: Flushes the bacterial irritants from the bladder, removing the noxious trigger that is driving the central nervous system into overdrive, leading to a steady reduction in the leg spasms over the next two days (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Catheter care nursing requested for review.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: GP review in 48-72 hours to review urine culture results and taper down the increased anti-spasmodic medications back to baseline.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the fever spikes dangerously high, if the patient develops severe shivering/rigors, or if the spasms begin to compromise breathing or chest expansion.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe spasms are a clinical symptom of your nervous system reacting to an infection. Treating the infection is the primary cure for the muscle rigidity. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The Valium will provide temporary, rapid relief from the cramping. As the antibiotics clear the bladder infection over the next 48 hours, the leg spasms will slowly retreat back to your manageable baseline.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care immediately if you develop a high fever with uncontrollable shaking, or if your chest muscles begin to spasm, making it difficult to breathe.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your spinal cord injury means your brain cannot effectively tell your leg muscles to relax. When your body develops an infection (like the UTI we suspect today), the pain signals drive your spinal cord into an overdrive state, causing these violent, rigid spasms. We are giving you a fast-acting muscle relaxant to stop the immediate pain, and antibiotics to eliminate the actual cause of the flare.
Actionable Steps: Do not attempt to forcefully pull or yank the legs straight; this only makes the spasm lock tighter. Use slow, steady pressure. Drink plenty of water to flush the bladder.

